          %h3#application_in_string_theory Application in string theory
          %p
            In
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/partition+functions"} partition functions
            tend to be automorphic forms for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/U-duality"} U-duality
            groups. See the
            %a{:href => "#ReferencesInStringTheory"} references below
          %h2#related_entries Related entries
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modular+form"}> modular form
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+modular+form"} topological modular form
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+automorphic+form"} topological automorphic form
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/automorphic+L-function"} automorphic L-function
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Langlands+duality"} Langlands duality
          %h2#references References
          %h3#general General
          %p Introductions and surveys include
          %ul
            %li#Deligne
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pierre+Deligne"}> Pierre Deligne
                ,
                %em
                  Fromed Modulaires et representations de
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi GL
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mn 2
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} GL(2)
                (
                %a{:href => "http://publications.ias.edu/sites/default/files/Number21.pdf"}>
                )
            %li#Gelbhart84
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stephen+Gelbart"}> Stephen Gelbart
                , starting on p. 20 (196) of
                = succeed "," do
                  %em An elementary introduction to the Langlands program
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.ams.org/journals/bull/1984-10-02/S0273-0979-1984-15237-6/"} web
            %li
              %p
                Nolan Wallach,
                %em Introductory lectures on automorphic forms
                (
                %a{:href => "http://math.ucsd.edu/~nwallach/luminy-port2.pdf"}> pdf
                )
            %li
              %p
                E. Kowalski, section 3 of
                %em Automorphic forms, L-functions and number theory (March 12–16) Three Introductory lectures
                (
                %a{:href => "http://www.math.ethz.ch/~kowalski/lectures.pdf"}> pdf
                )
            %li#GoldfeldHundley11
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dorian+Goldfeld"}> Dorian Goldfeld
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Joseph+Hundley"} Joseph Hundley
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Automorphic representations and L-functions for the general linear group
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://www.maths.nottingham.ac.uk/personal/ibf/text/gl2.pdf"} pdf
            %li#Bump
              %p
                Daniel Bump,
                %em Automorphic forms and representations
            %li#Loeffler11
              %p
                David Loeffler,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Computing with algebraic automorphic forms
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/LoefflerAutomorphic.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
            %li#Martin13
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kimball+Martin"}> Kimball Martin
                ,
                = succeed ",2013" do
                  %em A brief overview of modular and automorphic forms
                %a{:href => "http://www2.math.ou.edu/~kmartin/papers/mfs.pdf"} pdf
            %li#Litt
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Daniel+Litt"}> Daniel Litt
                ,
                %em Automorphic forms notes, part I
                (
                %a{:href => "http://math.stanford.edu/~dlitt/Talks/automorphicformspt1.pdf"}> pdf
                )
            %li
              %p
                Denis Trotabas, Modular Forms and Automorphic Representations (2010) (
                %a{:href => "http://math.stanford.edu/~conrad/modseminar/pdf/L10.pdf"}> pdf
                )
            %li
              %p
                Werner Muller, Spectral theory of automorphic forms (2010) (
                %a{:href => "http://www.math.uni-bonn.de/people/mueller/skripte/specauto.pdf"}> pdf
                )
            %li#Miyake76
              %p
                Toshitsune Miyake’s
                %em Modular Forms
                1976 (English version 1989) (
                %a{:href => "projecteuclid.org/euclid.bams/1183556263"}> review pdf
                )
          %p
            Review in the context of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+Langlands+correspondence"} geometric Langlands correspondence
            is in
          %ul
            %li#Frenkel05
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Edward+Frenkel"}> Edward Frenkel
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Lectures on the Langlands Program and Conformal Field Theory
              = succeed "," do
                %em Frontiers in number theory, physics, and geometry II
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/0512172"} arXiv:hep-th/0512172
          %p
            The generalization of theta functions to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/automorphic+forms"} automorphic forms
            is due to
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andr%C3%A9+Weil"}> André Weil
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Sur certaines groups d’operateur unitaires
          %p
            see
            %a{:href => "Langlands+program#Gelbhart84"} Gelbhart 84, page 35 (211)
            for review.
          %p Further developments here include
          %ul
            %li#Kudla77
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stephen+Kudla"}> Stephen Kudla
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Relations between automorphic forms produced by theta-functions
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Modular Functions of One Variable VI
            %li#Kudla78
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stephen+Kudla"}> Stephen Kudla
                ,
                = succeed ",Nagoya" do
                  %em Theta functions and Hilbert modular forms
            %li#Stopple95
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jeffrey+Stopple"}> Jeffrey Stopple
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    Theta and
                    = succeed "-function" do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mi L
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://matwbn.icm.edu.pl/ksiazki/aa/aa72/aa7221.pdf"} pdf
          %h3#ReferencesInStringTheory In string theory
          %p
            The relation between
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
            on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemann+surfaces"} Riemann surfaces
            and automorphic forms was first highlighted in
          %ul
            %li#Witten88
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Edward+Witten"}> Edward Witten
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Quantum field theory, Grassmannians, and algebraic curves
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://projecteuclid.org/euclid.cmp/1104160350"} Euclid
          %p See also
          %ul
            %li#GRV10
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+Green"}> Michael Green
              , Jorge G. Russo, Pierre Vanhove,
              %em Automorphic properties of low energy string amplitudes in various dimensions
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1001.2535"}> arXiv:1001.2535
              )
